<%# THE SACRIFICE VIEW (spec §9). `@presenter` is a SacrificePresenter.

    Reallocation cannot fix a budget that does not fit an income — "the app must say so rather than
    let someone reallocate through six months of sinking" — so this screen offers no move, no
    transfer and no distribution. It offers cuts, and the only links on it go to the rules' own
    edit forms, where a cut is actually made. NOTHING HERE IS WRITTEN (plan decision 3): the dial
    below is arithmetic in the browser over figures this page printed, and a reload returns the
    page to its opening state.

    THE WHOLE SCREEN IS DENOMINATED IN PER-PERIOD CLAIMS, and this is the one thing to know before
    editing it. Every figure — the gap, each row's amount, what a cut frees, the totals — is what
    the rule claims from ONE period (`Budget#steady_ask`). A rule's OWN unit is a different number:
    the demo's Rent rule is $1,500 a month and claims $692.31 of a biweekly period. Each row prints
    its rule's unit beside the claim so the edit form it links to is not a surprise, but nothing on
    this page ever adds a figure in the rule's unit to a figure in the period's. %>
<% content_for :title, "What could you cut?" %>

<%= page_header(title: "What could you cut?",
                subtitle: "Your rules ask for more than you bring in. This is what would have to give.") %>

<div class="space-y-6"
     data-controller="app--sacrifice--dial"
     data-app--sacrifice--dial-gap-value="<%= @presenter.gap_param %>">
  <%# §9'S HEADLINE, verbatim in shape: the gap, then the two figures it came from. The same two
      readers the Budget page's structural check prints (`Budget.steady_need` and the declared
      income), so a user arriving here from that page's button meets the number they just read. %>
  <section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded p-6">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-status-danger" data-figure="gap">
      <%= number_to_currency(@presenter.gap) %> underwater every period
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
      Your rules need <strong data-figure="rules-need"><%= number_to_currency(@presenter.rules_need) %></strong>.
      You typically bring home <strong data-figure="typical-income"><%= number_to_currency(@presenter.typical_income) %></strong>.
    </p>

    <%# THE UNWINNABLE CASE, STATED PLAINLY AND FIRST (spec §9: "if every available cut still
        leaves a gap, the app says so instead of offering false comfort").

        It is the SERVER's sentence and not the dial's, because it is a fact about the rules rather
        than about what is currently checked: it is true before the user touches anything and stays
        true whatever they touch. The dial still works underneath it — a user who cannot win still
        wants to see how close they can get, and hiding the list would leave the page saying only
        "no" — but the page OPENS on the sentence, with the number, so no arrangement of checkboxes
        can be mistaken for a way out.

        `unclosable` is the gap left after cutting every cuttable rule to zero. That is not a
        realistic budget and is not offered as one; it is the strongest claim available, and a
        softer ceiling would be the false comfort the spec names. %>
    <% if @presenter.unwinnable? %>
      <div class="mt-4 rounded border border-status-danger bg-status-danger-light px-4 py-3" data-unwinnable>
        <%# "every rule YOU CAN CUT", not "every rule below": on the all-anchored screen there is no
            rule below, and the sentence sat directly above an empty state reading "There is nothing
            here to cut" — one block arguing with the next. The scope has to name the cuttable set
            either way, because dropping it outright ("cutting every rule to nothing") would be
            FALSE on that same screen: cutting the anchored rules would close the gap, which is
            exactly what the user cannot do. %>
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-status-danger">
          Cutting every rule you can cut to nothing still leaves you
          <%= number_to_currency(@presenter.unclosable) %> underwater every period.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-status-danger">
          This budget does not fit this income. Nothing on this page can close it — the answer is a
          bigger income or a bill that goes away, and we would rather say so than have you
          rearrange it for six months.
        </p>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <%# THE LIST: what you could cut, then what you could not, then the running total — the shape
      §9 draws. One list rather than two sections, because the two groups together ARE the figure
      at the top of the page (SacrificePresenter#rows_total is `rules_need`, and the presenter spec
      pins it), and a reader who adds the column up must land on the number in the headline. %>
  <section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded">
    <div class="px-6 py-4 border-b border-gray-200">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">What could you cut?</h3>
      <%# ** THE ORDER IS PART OF THE OFFER, SO IT IS SAID (fix wave — LOW-3). ** `SacrificePresenter
          #rows` sorts `[-claim, owner name, id]` — the biggest claim first, because the fastest way
          to close a gap is at the top — and the page said nothing about it. A list of money with an
          unexplained order reads as arbitrary, and a reader who assumes alphabetical or "as entered"
          draws the wrong conclusion from where their own rules sit. %>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500" data-cut-list-order>
        Tick a rule and type what you would keep it at, per period. Nothing is saved — this is a
        question, not a change. The link on each row is where the rule is actually edited. The
        biggest claim is first, so the cuts that close the gap fastest are at the top.
      </p>
    </div>

    <% if @presenter.cuttable_rows.empty? %>
      <%# REACHABLE, and it is the worst version of the unwinnable case rather than an empty state:
          every rule the user has is anchored, so there is nothing on this screen to dial at all.
          The block above has already said the gap cannot be closed here; this says why the list
          below has no checkboxes in it. %>
      <div class="px-6 py-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">
          Every rule you have is anchored to a date, and a dated bill is not a rate you can dial
          down — the bill is what it is. There is nothing here to cut.
        </p>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <% @presenter.cuttable_rows.each do |row| %>
      <%# `data-claim` in plain, undelimited digits (see Row#claim_param), because `parseFloat` is
          what reads it: a $1,500-a-period rule delimited as "1,500.00" parses as 1.5, and the row
          would offer to free a dollar fifty out of fifteen hundred with every figure on the page
          still looking right. %>
      <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2"
           data-sacrifice-row="<%= row.budget.id %>"
           data-app--sacrifice--dial-target="row"
           data-claim="<%= row.claim_param %>">
        <input type="checkbox"
               id="cut-<%= row.budget.id %>"
               class="form-checkbox"
               aria-label="Cut <%= budget_rule_name(row.budget) %>"
               data-app--sacrifice--dial-target="toggle"
               data-action="change->app--sacrifice--dial#recompute">

        <label class="flex-1 min-w-40 text-sm font-medium text-gray-900" for="cut-<%= row.budget.id %>">
          <%= budget_rule_name(row.budget) %>
        </label>

        <%# THE CLAIM, AND THE RULE'S OWN UNIT WHERE IT IS A DIFFERENT THING. "$692.31 a period" is
            what this page adds up; "$1,500.00 a month" is what the edit form on the right of this
            row will show. A row printing only the first sends the user to a form whose number they
            do not recognise, and a row printing only the second invites them to subtract a monthly
            figure from a per-period gap. `sacrifice_rule_unit` is silent on a per-period rule,
            whose unit IS the period — see its comment for what that clause looked like. %>
        <span class="text-sm text-gray-600 tabular-nums" data-role="claim">
          <%= number_to_currency(row.claim) %> a period
          <span class="text-gray-400"><%= sacrifice_rule_unit(row) %></span>
        </span>

        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">cut to</span>
          <%# `step` and `min` match what a cut can be. The dial clamps anyway (see #freedBy) —
              a typed figure above the claim frees nothing and a blank one frees the lot — because
              a browser constraint is a rendering and the arithmetic must be right without it. %>
          <input type="number"
                 id="cut-amount-<%= row.budget.id %>"
                 class="form-input w-28 tabular-nums"
                 step="0.01"
                 min="0"
                 value="<%= row.claim_param %>"
                 aria-label="Cut <%= budget_rule_name(row.budget) %> to"
                 data-app--sacrifice--dial-target="amount"
                 <%# `change` AS WELL AS `input`, and it is a backstop rather than a duplicate.
                     `input` covers every keystroke, which is the whole of what a user typing does —
                     measured: typing "100" and then backspacing it away moves the total on each
                     press. It does NOT cover a value SET without keystrokes, and the field has at
                     least two such paths: a programmatic clear (which is exactly what Capybara's
                     `fill_in with: ""` does, and it left the total stale while the box read empty),
                     and a `type=number` input silently refusing a pasted "1,800". `change` fires on
                     blur in both, so the figure cannot stay stale once the user looks away.
                     #recompute reads the whole DOM every time, so firing twice costs a re-read and
                     can never double-count. %>
                 data-action="input->app--sacrifice--dial#recompute change->app--sacrifice--dial#recompute">
        </div>

        <%# "frees $100" is §9's own row copy, and the word is what makes the figure readable: a
            bare "$300.00" in a column of its own, beside a "$400.00 a period" and a "100", is a
            fourth number with nothing saying which of the three it came from. Outside the target
            span, because the dial writes `textContent` and would wipe a word it shared with. %>
        <span class="text-sm text-gray-600 tabular-nums w-32 text-right" data-role="row-frees">
          frees <span data-app--sacrifice--dial-target="rowFrees"><%= number_to_currency(0) %></span>
        </span>

        <%= link_to "Edit the rule",
                    edit_budget_path(row.budget),
                    class: "btn btn-secondary whitespace-nowrap",
                    data: { rule_edit_link: true } %>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# UNCUTTABLE RULES ARE MARKED AS SUCH (spec §9: "pretending rent is optional would be a
        lie"). They are listed rather than hidden because the gap at the top of this page is made
        of them too, and a user who cannot see them has been shown a list that does not add up. %>
    <% @presenter.fixed_rows.each do |row| %>
      <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 bg-gray-50"
           data-fixed-row="<%= row.budget.id %>">
        <span class="flex-1 min-w-40 text-sm font-medium text-gray-700">
          <%= budget_rule_name(row.budget) %>
        </span>
        <span class="text-sm text-gray-600 tabular-nums" data-role="claim">
          <%= number_to_currency(row.claim) %> a period
          <span class="text-gray-400"><%= sacrifice_rule_unit(row) %></span>
        </span>
        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500" data-role="fixed-reason">
          <%= sacrifice_fixed_reason(row) %>
        </span>
        <%= link_to "Edit the rule",
                    edit_budget_path(row.budget),
                    class: "btn btn-secondary whitespace-nowrap",
                    data: { rule_edit_link: true } %>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# THE RUNNING TOTAL. Server-rendered at the state the page opens in — nothing cut, so nothing
        freed and the whole gap still open — and recomputed by the dial's `connect()` before the
        user touches anything, so the two descriptions of "what is checked right now" agree from
        the first paint rather than from the first click. %>
    <div class="px-6 py-4 border-t-2 border-gray-200 space-y-1">
      <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4 text-sm">
        <span class="text-gray-600">Cutting frees</span>
        <%# The money is its OWN element and " a period" sits outside it, because the dial writes
            `textContent` — a target spanning both would have the unit wiped on the first keystroke
            and the figure would silently lose the only thing that says what it is per. %>
        <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900 tabular-nums" data-figure="frees">
          <span data-app--sacrifice--dial-target="freed"><%= number_to_currency(0) %></span> a period
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4 text-sm">
        <span class="text-gray-600">Where that leaves you</span>
        <span class="font-semibold tabular-nums text-status-danger"
              data-figure="verdict"
              data-app--sacrifice--dial-target="verdict">
          Still underwater <%= number_to_currency(@presenter.gap) %> a period
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
